# rig's systemd --user unit (PLAN.md section 5l).
#
# THIS UNIT DELIBERATELY HAS NO ExecStop, AND THE ABSENCE IS LOAD-BEARING.
# Do not add one. cmd/rigd/unit_test.go fails if anybody does, because a rule
# whose only enforcement is a comment is the thing this repo keeps paying for.
#
# THE SCAR, and it was paid for on this machine rather than read about.
# AgentBox's unit carried ExecStop=agentbox quit. agentbox is single-instance
# by flock and auto-spawns on first use, so `agentbox daemon` prints "already
# running" and exits 0 when one is up. systemd sees a Type=simple main process
# exit successfully, decides the service finished, and runs ExecStop - which
# killed the healthy daemon that was already serving. Enabling the unit left
# the desktop with no agentbox at all.
#
# THE CONCLUSION STANDS AND TWO OF ITS SUPPORTS DO NOT. CORRECTED 2026-09-16,
# both by measurement, because this paragraph claimed rig "has the identical
# shape" and cited a section for a property that section does not carry.
#
# NOT IDENTICAL, AND IT IS THE LOAD-BEARING HALF: agentbox exits 0 when an
# incumbent is up (verified at its source - cmd/agentbox/daemon.go calls
# os.Exit(exitOK) on ErrAlreadyRunning). rigd has never exited 0 there. It
# exited 1 and now exits 8, so the mechanism that caused the scar - systemd
# reading a successful exit as the service finishing - cannot occur here.
#
# AND SECTION 5g DOES NOT SPECIFY AUTO-SPAWN. It is "When rig is not running",
# and what it specifies is the opposite posture: a TOLERANT client with a
# reconnect loop, a resolved snapshot on disk and lifecycle notices, with the
# old fallback deleted. Nothing in it spawns a daemon, and no spawn path
# exists in the tree. The citation was inherited, not checked.
#
# SO WHY NO ExecStop ANYWAY, on grounds that survive both corrections: section
# 5f is a single-instance flock, section 18's SIGTERM path is already the
# graceful drain a stop command would perform, and an ExecStop can only
# duplicate it or race it. The 3-to-0 ruling in section 37 rests on section
# 5l's own text rather than on the shape argument above. This holds for
# `rig down` too: the lesson is not "no signal to a pid", it is no stop
# command in any form. `agentbox quit` was not a signal either.
#
# IT MANAGES production ONLY, and it now says so IN THE FLAG. CORRECTED
# 2026-09-16, by measurement. This is the THIRD unsupported claim in this
# header; two were corrected at bbd56cf and this one survived both passes.
#
# THE SUPERSEDED TEXT CONFLATED A PATH FACT WITH A NAME FACT. It read: "it
# sets no XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and passes no --estate, so it inherits the ordinary
# environment and reaches the default runtime directory, WHICH IS
# PRODUCTION'S." The two halves are independent in the code and only the
# first one was true.
#
# MEASURED through the MCP door against this exact binary, unnamed arm vs
# named arm, both restarted once:
#
#	no --estate    name:"" role:"unnamed"    epoch:0 -> 0
#	--estate=production  name:"production"   epoch:1 -> 2
#
# So a unit passing no --estate does not start production. It starts the
# UNNAMED estate, which claims no name, opens no coord.db, and carries a
# constant epoch 0 (cmd/rigd/main.go:165 gates the whole named branch on
# `*estate != ""`, and :156 says zero is the honest value for that case).
# internal/paths/RuntimeDir never reads an estate name, so nothing anywhere
# makes the default runtime directory "production".
#
# WHAT THAT COST, and it is why the flag is here rather than a comment: an
# unnamed estate's epoch cannot distinguish one daemon from the next, so a
# seat silently reattached to a restarted daemon could not have detected it
# from the epoch under this unit. The detector could not have gone red.
#
# THE OTHER HALF OF PRECONDITION 5 SURVIVES UNCHANGED AND IS STILL THE
# REASON THIS UNIT SETS NO XDG_RUNTIME_DIR: development is always placed
# explicitly in its own runtime directory, so a unit that sets none cannot
# reach it. That half was always about the path, and the path argument is
# sound. Naming the estate does not weaken it - it pins precondition 5 twice,
# by construction AND by flag, instead of resting on a name nothing assigns.
#
# STARTING IT WHILE A DAEMON IS ALREADY UP USED TO LOOP FOREVER, and the
# paragraph that stood here was wrong on both of its claims. It called this
# "one wrinkle, inherited and accepted" and said the unit reports inactive
# while the incumbent keeps serving. MEASURED 2026-09-16 against a running
# daemon: the unit reports activating (auto-restart), restarts every 2 s, and
# does not stop - 14 restarts in 28 seconds, about five journal lines each.
#
# The start rate limiter does not catch it. RestartSec=2 against the default
# StartLimitBurst=5 over StartLimitIntervalSec=10s puts the fifth restart at
# the edge of the window rather than inside it, so the limiter never fires.
# Both settings below are widened for that reason, and they bound any crash
# loop rather than only this one.
#
# THE REFUSAL IS NOT A FAULT, so it must not be retried. rigd exits
# exitAlreadyRunning (8, in cmd/rigd/main.go) when an incumbent holds the
# runtime directory or the estate name, and RestartPreventExitStatus tells
# systemd that status means "do not come back". A real crash still exits 1 and
# is still restarted, which is what Restart=on-failure is here for.
#
# WHY IT IS A THIRD NUMBER rather than 0 or 1: exiting 0 is the agentbox
# defect in the header above, where systemd read a refusal as the service
# finishing. Exiting 1 is this one. cmd/rigd/unit_test.go ties the number here
# to the constant in the Go source so the two cannot drift apart.
#
# AND THE CASE IS NOT RARE. The comment above assumed that at login nothing is
# running yet. A development estate takes the DEFAULT runtime directory unless
# XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is placed explicitly, so a development rigd left up is
# exactly the incumbent this hits - which is what was running when it was
# measured.

[Unit]
Description=rig, the estate platform
Documentation=https://github.com/borismilner/rig

# graphical-session.target, not default.target: rig needs the session bus and
# the display for section 12's toasts and section 11's tray.
PartOf=graphical-session.target
After=graphical-session.target

# In [Unit], not [Service]. systemd moved these and still accepts the old
# placement - by printing a warning, which cmd/rigd/unit_test.go reads as a
# failure because it gates on empty output rather than on the exit code.
StartLimitIntervalSec=60
StartLimitBurst=5

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/rigd --estate=production
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=2

# 8 is exitAlreadyRunning in cmd/rigd/main.go. Keep the two in step. The
# number is above the LSB range on purpose: systemd names lower ones from
# that table and would print NOTIMPLEMENTED beside a refusal that is neither.
RestartPreventExitStatus=8

[Install]
WantedBy=graphical-session.target
